nase gene\" get Modifier, while the disease uses stay SpecificDisease.\n\nI'm writing the rationale for the \"DM\" abbreviation, noting it stands for myotonic dystrophy and is typed as a synonymous mention.\n\nI'm drafting the JSON for the entity decisions and have reached the rationale for the \"DM\" abbreviation, which I'm treating as a synonymous mention of myotonic dystrophy, so it gets its own specific disease label. The entry for the first candidate is still being written.\n\nFinishing the JSON for the remaining candidates, starting with the third one, the \"DM\" abbreviation for myotonic dystrophy.\n\nI'm finishing the JSON entries for the DM mentions, marking the abbreviation and the disease uses as SpecificDisease and the gene and population uses as Modifiers.\n\nI'm marking the remaining entries: \"Fragile X syndrome\" as a specific disease, \"DM\" as the disease subject in the founder-effect sentence, and \"congenital DM\" as a specific disease form.\n\nI'm now assigning the last few DM candidates, 10 through 14: candidate 10 is a specific disease for myotonic dystrophy in the anticipation context, while the DM mentions attached to \"locus,\" \"kinase gene,\" and \"mutation\" are modifiers.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "{\"decisions\":[{\"id\":1,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'myotonic dystrophy' modifies the noun 'mutation' (the myotonic dystrophy mutation), so the disease name is used as a modifier of another noun phrase.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 3: Disease mentions used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":2,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'myotonic dystrophy' is named as the disease caused by the CTG repeat mutation. It is a single specific disease with no further categorization in this context.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 1: Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":3,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'DM' is the abbreviation of myotonic dystrophy and is annotated as a separate synonymous mention of the specific disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 6: Synonymous mentions (abbreviations)\"},{\"id\":4,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'DM' modifies the noun 'population' (global DM population), so it functions as a disease-name modifier of another noun phrase.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 3: Disease mentions used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":5,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'DM' modifies 'kinase gene' (DM kinase gene) and is used as a modifier of another noun phrase rather than as a standalone disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 3: Disease mentions used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":6,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'DM' is the grammatical subject referring to the disease itself ('DM is a consequence of one or few ancestral mutations'), so it is a specific disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 1: Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":7,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'Fragile X syndrome' names one specific disease with no further categorization.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 1: Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":8,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'DM' is the subject referring to the disease ('DM exhibited a founder effect'), a specific disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 1: Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":9,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'congenital DM' denotes a particular congenital form of myotonic dystrophy, analogous to a particular disease form that includes its qualifier, so the full string is the minimum necessary span.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate section 5: Minimum necessary span of text; Examples item 15\"},{\"id\":10,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'myotonic dystrophy' is named as the disease in the genetic anticipatio